    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Привет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi.' In Russian, 'hi' leads into 'hi' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Привет, Макс.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hi, Max.' Notice the word order: 'hi' comes first, and 'Max' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Как дела?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How are you?' Sasha puts 'how' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'things'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Бывало и лучше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I've been better.' The key word here means 'to happen', which Max combines with 'better'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Всё ещё переживаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you still worrying?' The key word here means 'more', which Sasha combines with 'to worry'. Max answers straight away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, no.' Max starts with 'yes' and pairs it with 'no' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С Викой всё в прошлом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'With Vika everything is in the past.' Max puts 'Vika' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'last'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ты уверен?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you sure?' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'you' straight to 'sure'. Then Max replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course.' The first content word means 'of course', and 'of course' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Ну ладно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, okay.' A compact expression built around 'oh' and 'all right'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "sasha",
      "t": "Мне пора работать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It's time for me to work.' Listen for the word meaning 'to me' right alongside 'it's time' in this line. And Max jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Увидимся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'See you.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'will see each other' turns the action back onto the subject. Now let us listen to the conversation once again.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
